EXPERIMENT 9
Creating relationships between databases and performing Joins, Sub-queries

AIM
To create related tables in a relational database using primary and foreign keys, and to retrieve related data using different JOIN operations and sub-queries.

OBJECTIVES
• Create tables with primary-key and foreign-key constraints.
• Understand one-to-many relationships.
• Perform INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, and RIGHT JOIN operations.
• Use aliases and join conditions.
• Write single-row, multi-row, and correlated sub-queries.
• Compare JOIN-based and sub-query-based approaches.

THEORY
A primary key uniquely identifies a row. A foreign key refers to a primary key in another table and maintains referential integrity. JOIN combines related rows. A sub-query is a query nested inside another query and may return a single value, multiple values, or a result set.

RESULT
The program demonstrates relationships, JOIN operations, and sub-queries using Department, Student, Course, and Enrollment tables.

